% =============================================================================
% ABSTRACT
% =============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
School Services Center (SSC) platforms and similar regulated payment intermediaries coordinate fiat collection across multiple institutions, yet they often suffer from a structural mismatch between off-chain settlement finality and the absence of independently verifiable reconciliation evidence. Many blockchain-based triple-entry accounting (TEA) instantiations conflate value settlement and evidentiary anchoring, or assume that tokenized settlement is permissible. This assumption is often inappropriate in regulated fiat environments such as K-12 educational finance. This paper proposes DR-TEA, a fiat-anchored dual-rail architecture that separates regulated value transfer from a public evidentiary layer, and introduces \emph{reconciliation finality} as a mechanism-independent property. To address selective omission before anchoring, we design an omission-aware protocol using signed receipts, monotonic batch chaining, counterparty co-signing, and redundant transparency monitoring. We evaluate the architecture through SSC-ReconBench, an open benchmark comprising synthetic K-12 and secondary-domain payment workloads, fault injection, omission attacks, SLA sensitivity, live Cardano testnet experiments, a limited mainnet pilot, and separated model-based cost baselines. Preliminary experimental placeholders will be replaced after execution; the evaluation pipeline targets sub-millisecond local verification, low batched anchoring cost, measurable omission detection, and explicit false-positive reporting. The study contributes a reusable architectural pattern, formal reconciliation properties, an omission-aware protocol, and an open benchmark for compliance-bound payment ecosystems.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Triple-entry accounting, blockchain reconciliation, Cardano, K-12 finance, reconciliation finality, omission resistance, Merkle commitments, regulated payments, design science, transparency logs.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =============================================================================
% I. INTRODUCTION
% =============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{S}{chool} Services Center (SSC) platforms coordinate tuition and fee collection across multiple educational institutions~\cite{le2026ssc}. In such ecosystems, funds settle through regulated banking channels, while payment-status information propagates through asynchronous callbacks, delayed reconciliation files, and heterogeneous wallet notifications. The resulting friction is not primarily a payment-rail failure. It is an evidentiary failure: authorized parties cannot independently verify whether a payment event was recorded, included in a committed batch, ordered chronologically, or selectively omitted.

Triple-entry accounting (TEA) has been proposed as a mechanism for reducing this ambiguity by introducing a cryptographically verifiable third entry shared across organizational boundaries~\cite{grigg2024tea, ibanez2025tea}. Surveys indicate that much of the TEA literature remains conceptual or focused on high-level accounting transformation~\cite{sarwar2023blockchains, sarwar2026survey, joseph2023tea}. At the same time, several blockchain-native TEA prototypes and payment-oriented designs conflate, either explicitly or implicitly, the settlement of value and the anchoring of evidence. We do not claim that all TEA literature requires value-information co-location. Rather, we observe that many crypto-native instantiations are difficult to deploy in regulated fiat environments where settlement must remain within banking rails.

This creates an applicability paradox. The domains that suffer most from reconciliation fragmentation---multi-party educational finance, tuition collection, institutional fee processing---are often those where tokenized settlement is legally or operationally inappropriate. Centralized databases can synchronize state but do not provide independent public tamper-evidence. Public blockchains can provide tamper-evidence but are often proposed in forms that conflict with fiat-native compliance.

This paper addresses that gap. We propose DR-TEA, a fiat-anchored dual-rail triple-entry architecture in which regulated fiat settlement remains off-chain, while canonicalized payment evidence is batched into Merkle commitments and anchored on a public blockchain. The architecture is designed not as a payment rail, but as a reconciliation evidence layer.

Compared with our conference version~\cite{le2026ssc}, this journal version makes the following extensions.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
    \item We introduce \emph{reconciliation finality} as a mechanism-independent property and separate it from settlement finality, consensus finality, and the specific receipt mechanism used to achieve it.
    \item We design an omission-aware pre-anchoring protocol using signed receipts, counterparty co-signing, fallback provisional receipts, and redundant transparency monitors.
    \item We expand the threat model to include collusion, monitor failure, proof-API distrust, reorganization handling, and fallback operation.
    \item We introduce SSC-ReconBench, a benchmark with fault injection, omission attacks, false-positive measurement, SLA sensitivity, and a secondary-domain transferability workload.
    \item We separate live empirical baselines from model-based estimates to avoid methodological asymmetry.
    \item We provide a reproducibility package with dry-run mode, configuration-controlled experiments, and artifact release.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:theory} formalizes reconciliation finality. Section~\ref{sec:arch} presents the architecture. Section~\ref{sec:omission} specifies the omission-aware protocol. Section~\ref{sec:prototype} describes the prototype. Section~\ref{sec:exp} details the experimental design. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports experimental placeholders to be filled after execution. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses implications, compliance, and fallback. Section~\ref{sec:threats} addresses threats to validity. Section~\ref{sec:diff} summarizes differences from the conference paper. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

% =============================================================================
% II. RELATED WORK
% =============================================================================
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Triple-Entry Accounting}
Grigg~\cite{grigg2024tea} describes the third entry as a cryptographically verifiable shared receipt across organizational boundaries. Sarwar et al.~\cite{sarwar2023blockchains, sarwar2026survey} survey blockchain-based TEA and observe that many studies remain conceptual. Pan et al.~\cite{pan2023private} propose a private TEA protocol on Bitcoin, while Faccia et al.~\cite{faccia2020blockchain} emphasize blockchain hashes as an accounting axis. Iba\~{n}ez et al.~\cite{ibanez2025tea} discuss terminology standardization. These works motivate TEA but provide limited treatment of regulated fiat constraints, selective omission, and deployable benchmarking.

\subsection{Transparency Logs and Timestamping}
Certificate Transparency~\cite{laurie2013rfc6962} and CONIKS~\cite{melara2015coniks} demonstrate that append-only logs, signed timestamps, and independent monitors can detect omission or misissuance. OpenTimestamps~\cite{toddtimestamps} and commercial services such as OriginStamp~\cite{originstamp} use blockchain anchoring to timestamp hashes. These systems are technically related to our evidence layer. However, they do not target multi-party fiat payment reconciliation, canonical payment-event modeling, institutional co-signing, or reconciliation-specific dispute workflows.

\subsection{Enterprise Immutable Logs and Financial Ledgers}
Centralized immutable ledgers such as Amazon QLDB~\cite{amazonqldb} and appendable log platforms such as Apache Kafka~\cite{apachekafka} provide tamper-evident sequencing within a single administrative domain. Permissioned financial platforms such as Corda~\cite{brown2016corda} and Hyperledger Fabric~\cite{androulaki2018fabric} support institutional workflows but generally do not provide public independent verifiability without consortium membership. Cross-chain tooling such as Hyperledger Cactus~\cite{hyperledgercactus} is relevant to interoperability but not directly to fiat-native reconciliation evidence.

\subsection{Payment Channels and Finality Literature}
State channels and payment channel networks separate off-chain activity from on-chain settlement~\cite{poon2016lightning, gudgeon2020sok}. They demonstrate that settlement and evidence can be decoupled, but typically assume crypto-collateralized channels rather than regulated fiat settlement. Classical distributed-systems work on time, ordering, and transaction processing~\cite{lamport1978time, gray1992transaction} provides the conceptual foundation for finality and consistency, while payment messaging standards such as ISO~20022~\cite{iso20022} shape reconciliation workflows in banking.

