% =============================================================================
% III. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK
% =============================================================================
\section{Theoretical Framework}
\label{sec:theory}

\subsection{Value Layer and Information Layer}
We distinguish two functions often conflated in blockchain payment literature.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Definition 1 (Value Layer).} The Value Layer comprises legal and financial infrastructure that transfers monetary claims. Its properties are settlement finality, regulatory compliance, custodianship, and dispute handling through banking and legal mechanisms.
\end{quote}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Definition 2 (Information Layer).} The Information Layer comprises systems that generate, transmit, store, and verify payment-status evidence. Its properties are consistency, timeliness, auditability, and independent verifiability.
\end{quote}

We do not claim that these layers are always independent in all systems. Rather, we make the following design assumption.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Assumption A1 (Evidence-Settlement Separability).} For the regulated payment setting considered here, cryptographic evidence integrity can be provided without modifying the fiat settlement rail. This assumption is evaluated empirically through decoupling experiments in Section~\ref{sec:exp}.
\end{quote}

\subsection{Canonical Payment Events}
Let $\mathcal{I}$ be a set of invoices, $\mathcal{P}$ a set of authorized parties, and $\mathcal{E}$ a set of payment events. A canonical event is:
\begin{equation}
e = (\mathit{inv\_id}, \mathit{payer\_ref}, \mathit{amount}, \mathit{currency},
\mathit{status}, \mathit{channel}, \mathit{ts}, \mathit{idem\_key})
\end{equation}
where $\mathit{idem\_key}$ is an idempotency key. The canonical hash is:
\begin{equation}
h = H(C(e))
\end{equation}
where $C(\cdot)$ is deterministic canonicalization and $H$ is SHA-256.

\subsection{Reconciliation Fault}
Let $S_k(i,t)$ be the state of invoice $i$ observed by party $k$ at time $t$. A reconciliation fault exists when:
\begin{equation}
\exists i \in \mathcal{I}, \exists k_1 \neq k_2 \in \mathcal{P}:
S_{k_1}(i,t) \neq S_{k_2}(i,t)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Reconciliation Finality}
To avoid circularity, we define reconciliation finality independently of any specific mechanism.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Definition 3 (Reconciliation Finality).} A payment event $e$ achieves reconciliation finality for authorized parties $\mathcal{P}$ under assumptions $\mathcal{A}$ when the following properties can be established:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item \emph{Inclusion}: $e$ can be shown to belong to a committed evidence set.
    \item \emph{Ordering}: the position of $e$ relative to committed evidence is determinate.
    \item \emph{Consistency}: no conflicting committed evidence set is accepted for the same sequence context.
    \item \emph{Non-repudiation}: authorized parties can present cryptographic evidence to a third party.
    \item \emph{Detectable absence}: if $e$ was acknowledged but not committed within a bounded interval, at least one authorized party can produce evidence of non-commitment under $\mathcal{A}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{quote}

Definition~3 is deliberately mechanism-independent. Signed receipts, co-signing, monitors, Merkle batching, and blockchain anchoring are possible mechanisms for satisfying it.

\subsection{Assumptions}
We state the assumptions under which the architecture is evaluated.

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item \textbf{A2 (Hash collision resistance).} $H$ is collision-resistant.
    \item \textbf{A3 (Substrate persistence).} The anchoring substrate preserves confirmed commitments beyond a chosen confirmation threshold, except under major consensus failure.
    \item \textbf{A4 (Authenticated ingestion).} Event ingestion endpoints are authenticated, and signing keys are protected.
    \item \textbf{A5 (At least one honest witness).} For omission detectability, at least one relevant receipt witness or monitor is honest and available within the SLA.
    \item \textbf{A6 (Bounded delay).} Network and operational delays are bounded sufficiently for SLA-based monitoring.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Design Claim}
We avoid presenting the following as a theorem.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Design Claim 1.} Under assumptions A1--A6, and absent full collusion of all relevant witnesses and monitors, DR-TEA can satisfy the five properties of reconciliation finality for canonical payment events. The claim is supported by property-based testing, fault injection, omission simulation, and live testnet/mainnet pilots, not by a formal impossibility or security-reduction proof.
\end{quote}

\subsection{Detectability Bound}
Let $p_w$ be the probability that at least one honest witness issues a receipt for an acknowledged event, and $p_m$ the probability that at least one monitor detects an unmet SLA. A simplified upper bound for omission detection is:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{ODR} \leq p_w p_m
\label{eq:odr-bound}
\end{equation}
Equation~\ref{eq:odr-bound} explains why residual non-detection is partly socio-technical: if all required witnesses are unavailable or collude, technical detection cannot guarantee completeness.

% =============================================================================
% IV. ARCHITECTURE
% =============================================================================
\section{DR-TEA Architecture}
\label{sec:arch}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{Figure1.png}
\caption{DR-TEA separates the regulated Value Layer from the Information Layer. Dashed components implement omission-aware monitoring and dispute generation.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Value Layer}
The Value Layer preserves existing banking and payment workflows. Parents initiate payments through heterogeneous channels. Funds settle into SSC collection accounts. No component of this layer is required to change for the proposed architecture.

\subsection{Information Layer}
The Information Layer contains six components.

\subsubsection{Ingestion Gateway}
The gateway receives authenticated bank/wallet notifications, applies schema validation, deduplication, canonicalization, and hashing. It produces canonical event hashes and forwards them to the receipt and batching services.

\subsubsection{Idempotency Key Specification}
To address reviewer concerns, we specify idempotency key generation as follows. If the payment provider supplies a globally unique transaction identifier, the gateway sets:
\begin{equation}
\mathit{idem\_key} = \mathit{provider\_tx\_id}
\end{equation}
Otherwise:
\begin{equation}
\mathit{idem\_key} =
\mathrm{Trunc}_{128}(\mathrm{HMAC\mbox{-}SHA256}(K_{\mathrm{ingest}}, \phi(e)))
\end{equation}
where $\phi(e)$ is a canonical context containing invoice identifier, channel, payer reference, amount, currency, and timestamp bucket. The key is stored with a uniqueness constraint. Front-running is mitigated by authenticated ingestion, provider webhook signatures, rate limiting, and audit logging. The idempotency key is not treated as a secret authorization token.

\subsubsection{Receipt Signing Service}
For each accepted event, the SSC gateway signs a receipt:
\begin{equation}
R_{\mathrm{SSC}}(e) = \mathrm{Sign}_{\mathrm{SSC}}(h \| \mathit{seq} \| \mathit{ts})
\end{equation}
where $\mathit{seq}$ is monotonic. Where operationally available, a school counterparty co-signs:
\begin{equation}
R_{\mathrm{school}}(e) = \mathrm{Sign}_{\mathrm{school}}(h \| \mathit{seq} \| \mathit{ts})
\end{equation}
Receipts are written to an append-only receipt log and made observable to independent monitors.

\subsubsection{Batch Commitment Engine}
Hashes are grouped into ordered batches $B_j = [h_1, \ldots, h_m]$. The batch root is computed with predecessor chaining:
\begin{equation}
r_j = \mathrm{MerkleRoot}(B_j \| r_{j-1})
\label{eq:chain-root}
\end{equation}
Equation~\ref{eq:chain-root} is a design choice favoring simplicity and easy auditability. We do not claim it is optimal. Alternative structures include hash chains, Merkle mountain ranges, and sequence-numbered accumulators.

\subsubsection{Anchoring Service}
The root $r_j$ is anchored to Cardano using CIP-20 metadata~\cite{cip20}. The eUTXO model provides deterministic transaction semantics suitable for discrete evidence commitments~\cite{pomogalova2022utxo, brunjes2020utxo}. The architecture is substrate-agnostic in principle; optional adapters may anchor the same root to other networks for redundancy or comparison.

\subsubsection{Proof Distribution and Direct Verification}
A Proof Distribution API provides convenience access to Merkle proofs. However, it is not a trust anchor. Clients can independently retrieve the anchored root and transaction metadata from public explorers or APIs such as Koios. The verifier recomputes:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Verify}(r_j, H(e), \pi_e) \in \{\mathrm{true}, \mathrm{false}\}
\end{equation}
Thus a malicious API can be detected if it returns proofs inconsistent with the public root.

\subsection{Triple-Entry Interpretation}
Entry~1 is the SSC collector-side record. Entry~2 is the school/obligation-side record. Entry~3 is the independently verifiable evidence derived from the anchored commitment and proof. The third entry narrows the trust boundary but does not replace institutional accounting systems.

% =============================================================================
% V. OMISSION-AWARE PROTOCOL
% =============================================================================
\section{Omission-Aware Protocol and Trust Model}
\label{sec:omission}

\subsection{Threat Model}
We consider a partially trusted operator environment.

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item \textbf{T1 Selective omission:} an operator acknowledges an event but excludes it from batching.
    \item \textbf{T2 Receipt suppression:} an operator avoids issuing a receipt.
    \item \textbf{T3 Monitor compromise:} a monitor fails to raise disputes.
    \item \textbf{T4 Proof API deception:} an API returns incorrect proofs or roots.
    \item \textbf{T5 Partial collusion:} SSC colludes with one school or one monitor.
    \item \textbf{T6 Full collusion:} all relevant witnesses and monitors collude.
    \item \textbf{T7 Chain reorganization:} an anchored commitment is reversed.
    \item \textbf{T8 Key compromise:} signing keys are stolen.
\end{itemize}

Full collusion (T6) is outside the scope of technical detection and requires institutional governance, legal controls, or external audit evidence such as bank statements.

\subsection{Protocol}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:omission} summarizes the omission-aware protocol.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\caption{Omission-Aware Pre-Anchoring Protocol}
\label{alg:omission}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Event $e$, SLA window $\Delta$, signing keys
\ENSURE Anchored proof or dispute record

\STATE Canonicalize and hash event: $h = H(C(e))$
\STATE Generate monotonic sequence $\mathit{seq}$
\STATE SSC signs receipt $R_{\mathrm{SSC}}$
\IF{school co-signer available}
    \STATE School signs receipt $R_{\mathrm{school}}$
\ELSE
    \STATE Mark event as \texttt{provisional\_no\_cosign}
\ENDIF
\STATE Append receipts to append-only receipt log
\STATE Add $h$ to pending batch $B_j$
\WHEN{batch closes}
    \STATE Compute $r_j$ using Eq.~\ref{eq:chain-root}
    \STATE Anchor $r_j$ on Cardano
    \STATE Distribute Merkle proofs
\ENDWHEN
\FOR{each monitor independently}
    \STATE Observe receipt log and anchored roots
    \IF{receipt older than $\Delta$ has no proof}
        \STATE Generate dispute $D(e, R_{\mathrm{SSC}}, R_{\mathrm{school}})$
        \STATE Notify authorized parties
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Co-Signing Unavailability}
If a school co-signer is unavailable, the system does not silently drop the event. Instead:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item SSC issues a provisional receipt with a missing co-signature flag.
    \item The event is included in a batch with a risk flag.
    \item Monitors produce warnings for all provisional events.
    \item Auditors sample provisional events at a higher rate.
    \item If co-signing remains unavailable beyond a policy window, a dispute is opened.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Monitor Redundancy}
To reduce monitor centralization, we recommend at least two independent monitors: one operated by SSC and one operated by an auditor, school consortium, or external observer. Monitors publish signed heartbeats. A dispute raised by any monitor is logged. Threshold dispute policies can be used when monitors disagree.

\subsection{Collusion Analysis}
Table~\ref{tab:collusion} summarizes detectability.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Collusion and failure scenarios. ``Detectable'' assumes at least one honest witness/monitor and available logs.}
\label{tab:collusion}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.1}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.1cm} p{2.1cm} p{2.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Scenario} & \textbf{Detectability} & \textbf{Mitigation} \\
\midrule
SSC alone omits event & High if receipt witness exists & Co-signing, monitor \\
SSC + one school collude & Medium & Parent/app receipt, bank statement audit \\
SSC + monitor collude & Medium & Independent monitor \\
School unavailable & Medium & Provisional receipts, sampling \\
All witnesses collude & Low & Governance/legal controls \\
Chain reorg & High after threshold & Re-anchor, fallback \\
Key compromise & Variable & Rotation, HSM/KMS, revocation \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{Figure2.png}
\caption{Omission-aware protocol sequence with redundant monitors and dispute generation.}
\label{fig:protocol}
\end{figure}


% =============================================================================
% VI. PROTOTYPE
% =============================================================================
\section{Prototype Implementation}
\label{sec:prototype}

\subsection{Technology Stack}
The prototype contains:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item Ingestion Gateway: Python 3.12, FastAPI, Pydantic v2, PostgreSQL, Redis.
    \item Anchoring Service: Node.js 20, TypeScript, Mesh SDK, Cardano Preprod, Koios API.
    \item Receipt/Monitor Service: Python 3.12, FastAPI, PostgreSQL, APScheduler.
    \item Verifier CLI: Python/TypeScript client with direct chain-query mode.
    \item Benchmark runner: Python, YAML configuration, CSV/Parquet outputs.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Dry-Run Mode}
To support reproducibility without testnet dependency, the anchoring adapter supports a deterministic dry-run mode. In dry-run mode:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item transactions are simulated;
    \item fees are estimated from a local configuration file;
    \item confirmation delays are sampled from configurable distributions;
    \item all outputs remain structurally identical to live mode.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Property-Based Testing}
We test the following properties:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item P1: Merkle root determinism.
    \item P2: valid proofs verify.
    \item P3: tampered leaves fail.
    \item P4: predecessor root modification invalidates chain.
    \item P5: duplicate idempotency keys are rejected.
    \item P6: malformed payloads are isolated before batching.
\end{itemize}

Results are reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}.

% =============================================================================
% VII. EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN
% =============================================================================
\section{Experimental Design: SSC-ReconBench}
\label{sec:exp}

\subsection{Workloads}
SSC-ReconBench includes two workloads.

\subsubsection{W1: K-12 SSC Workload}
A synthetic K-12 workload of 100{,}000 events calibrated to SSC operational patterns~\cite{le2026ssc}. It includes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item diurnal payment peaks;
    \item school-year enrollment bursts;
    \item duplicates;
    \item malformed payloads;
    \item refunds;
    \item partial payments;
    \item late callbacks.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{W2: Secondary-Domain Transferability Workload}
To address generalizability concerns, we include a synthetic secondary-domain workload of 50{,}000 events representing either university fee collection or utility bill reconciliation. The schema differs from W1 by adding domain-specific fields while retaining the canonical evidence core. This workload tests whether the architecture adapts without redesign.

\subsection{Baselines}
To avoid asymmetry identified by reviewers, baselines are separated into live empirical baselines and model-based estimates.

\subsubsection{Live Baselines}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item B1: Centralized signed receipt log.
    \item B2: Per-event Cardano anchoring, limited pilot plus extrapolation.
    \item B3: DR-TEA batched Cardano anchoring.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Model-Based Baselines}
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item B4: OpenTimestamps-style calendar anchoring.
    \item B5: Local CometBFT/Tendermint permissioned notary.
    \item B6: Ethereum L1 cost model.
    \item B7: Optional EVM L2 or Algorand adapter pilot.
\end{itemize}

B4--B7 are not directly compared with live latency metrics unless deployed in a pilot. Where simulated, they appear in a separate table with explicit caveats.

\subsection{Experiments}
Table~\ref{tab:experiments} lists experiments.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Experiment matrix.}
\label{tab:experiments}
