\subsection{Metrics}
Table~\ref{tab:metrics} defines metrics.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Metrics.}
\label{tab:metrics}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.1}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.2cm} p{2.2cm} p{4.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Definition} \\
\midrule
$L_{\mathrm{sub}}$ & Submission latency & Time from batch submit to receipt \\
$T_{\mathrm{ver}}$ & Verification time & Local proof verification \\
$S_{\pi}$ & Proof size & Serialized proof bytes \\
$\mathrm{TEF}$ & Time-to-evidence-finality & Batch close + submission + confirmation \\
$\mathrm{FIR}_{\mathrm{syn}}$ & Syntactic fault isolation & Malformed events blocked before batching \\
$\mathrm{FRR}$ & False rejection rate & Valid events wrongly rejected \\
$\mathrm{ODR}$ & Omission detection rate & True omitted events detected \\
$\mathrm{FPR}$ & False positive rate & Non-omitted events flagged \\
$\mathrm{MTTD}$ & Mean time to detect & Time from omission to dispute \\
$C_{1k}$ & Cost per 1k events & Estimated USD per 1{,}000 events \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Statistical Plan}
Simulation experiments are run with 30 seeds where feasible. Live testnet experiments are run with 10 repetitions where cost permits. Mainnet pilots are limited to 30--90 anchors due to cost and ethical considerations. We report mean, median, 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals, and p95/p99 tail metrics where relevant. Kruskal--Wallis tests are used for non-normal distributions, with Holm correction for multiple comparisons.

