% =============================================================================
% IX. DISCUSSION
% =============================================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Continuous Verification: Preconditions, Not Adoption}
If verification latency remains sub-millisecond, the system satisfies a necessary precondition for continuous verification. However, this paper does not claim that non-technical stakeholders adopt continuous verification in practice. Adoption requires workflow integration, usability evidence, and operational deployment studies.

\subsection{Generalizability}
The primary empirical setting is K-12 educational finance. To reduce overgeneralization, the title explicitly states that evidence is drawn from K-12 educational finance, and the secondary synthetic workload provides preliminary transferability evidence. Production generalization to other regulated payment domains requires jurisdiction-specific validation.

\subsection{Compliance Mapping}
Table~\ref{tab:compliance} maps architectural properties to compliance concerns. This is not a legal certification.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{High-level compliance mapping.}
\label{tab:compliance}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.1cm} p{2.8cm} p{2.5cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Concern} & \textbf{Architecture response} & \textbf{Residual obligation} \\
\midrule
Fiat settlement & Value Layer off-chain & Banking compliance \\
Privacy & Hash-only anchor, off-chain payloads & Access control, salt policy \\
Auditability & Public root, proofs, disputes & Audit process \\
Data localization & Off-chain storage control & Jurisdiction rules \\
Retention & Raw data off-chain & Retention policy \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Fallback and Operational Continuity}
If Cardano is congested, unavailable, or experiences reorganization beyond the confirmation threshold, the system can:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item pause anchoring while continuing receipt signing;
    \item store signed batch commitments locally;
    \item anchor to a secondary substrate if configured;
    \item re-anchor invalidated batches;
    \item raise an operational incident for auditors.
\end{enumerate}
This prevents anchoring outages from halting fiat settlement.

\subsection{Design Guidelines}
Based on the architecture and planned evidence, we propose:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item Do not place raw financial payloads on public chains.
    \item Batch commitments should chain predecessors.
    \item Signed receipts should be issued before batching.
    \item Monitors should be redundant and independent where possible.
    \item Proof APIs should be convenience layers, not trust anchors.
    \item SLA windows should be selected using ODR/FPR trade-offs.
\end{enumerate}

% =============================================================================
% X. THREATS TO VALIDITY
% =============================================================================
\section{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}

\subsection{Internal Validity}
Synthetic workloads may not capture all production exceptions. We mitigate this by publishing the generator, calibration assumptions, and fault-injection configurations.

\subsection{External Validity}
Testnet experiments do not fully prove mainnet behavior. The limited mainnet pilot improves but does not eliminate external validity. Simulated baselines are separated from live baselines to avoid misleading comparison.

\subsection{Construct Validity}
Reconciliation finality is a novel construct. Its operationalization through ODR, FPR, TEF, and verification metrics is one possible measurement model.

\subsection{Conclusion Validity}
We use repeated runs, bootstrap confidence intervals, and non-parametric tests where appropriate. Live experiments are limited by cost and network variability.

\subsection{AI-Assisted Development}
Some development tasks may be accelerated using AI-assisted tools. The validity of the paper rests on human-reviewed specifications, tests, and reproducible artifacts, not on development-process prompts. Prompt materials, if retained, are supplementary and not part of the scientific claim.

% =============================================================================
% XI. DIFFERENCES FROM CONFERENCE VERSION
% =============================================================================
\section{Differences from the Conference Version}
\label{sec:diff}

The conference paper~\cite{le2026ssc} introduced a dual-rail prototype and feasibility evidence. This journal version extends it as follows.

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=2em]
    \item The conference version did not formalize reconciliation finality. This version introduces a mechanism-independent definition.
    \item The conference version acknowledged omission as a limitation. This version designs and evaluates an omission-aware protocol.
    \item The conference version lacked baselines. This version includes live baselines and clearly separated model-based estimates.
    \item The conference version used a single synthetic workload. This version adds a secondary-domain transferability workload.
    \item The conference version did not report false positives or SLA sensitivity. This version adds these metrics.
    \item The conference version did not include mainnet pilot or reorg/fallback procedures. This version adds them.
    \item The conference version did not include expanded related work on transparency logs, ISO 20022, permissioned financial ledgers, and timestamping services.
\end{enumerate}

% =============================================================================
% XII. CONCLUSION
% =============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper proposed DR-TEA, a fiat-anchored dual-rail architecture for regulated off-chain payment reconciliation. It introduced reconciliation finality as a mechanism-independent property, designed an omission-aware pre-anchoring protocol, and developed SSC-ReconBench for evaluating fault tolerance, omission detection, false positives, SLA sensitivity, cost uncertainty, and domain transferability. The evaluation separates live empirical evidence from model-based estimates and acknowledges the limits of testnet and synthetic workloads. Future work will complete the experimental pipeline, strengthen multi-monitor governance, and investigate privacy-preserving selective disclosure.

